From: fanha99 <fanha99@hotmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nss_core: back firmware empty-buffer payloads with whole pages

The empty-buffer payloads come from kmalloc-4096 since "back firmware
empty-buffer payloads with kmalloc". That cache uses order-3 slabs of
eight objects, and a slab is only given back once all eight objects are
free. The firmware returns payloads in no particular order, so after
load the pool leaves many slabs partly used: on a 256MB IPQ5018 (Xiaomi
CR8806, 5GHz Wi-Fi offload) four iperf3 runs left 396 partial
kmalloc-4k slabs holding 2168 objects, ~4MB of empty objects that no
one could use. Every new slab also needs an order-3 block, which a
fragmented 256MB board does not always have in atomic context.

Give each payload a page of its own and build the skb around it. The
payload and its skb_shared_info fit in one page for every standard MRU
(2368 bytes with the 1984-byte buffer), so the cost per payload does
not change, and the page goes back to the page allocator as soon as
the skb is freed. Payloads that do not fit in a page (jumbo MRU) keep
using kmalloc.

Signed-off-by: fanha99 <fanha99@hotmail.com>
---
--- a/nss_core.c
+++ b/nss_core.c
@@ -2094,6 +2094,40 @@
 }
 
 /*
+ * nss_core_alloc_empty_buffer()
+ *	Allocate one payload for the firmware's empty-buffer pool.
+ *
+ * The firmware keeps these payloads for an unbounded time and hands them
+ * back in no particular order. A kmalloc-4096 object lives in an order-3
+ * slab of eight, and the slab is only released once all eight are free, so
+ * the pool's churn leaves partly used slabs behind (~1000 empty objects,
+ * ~4MB, on a 256MB IPQ5018 after a few iperf3 runs), and every new slab
+ * needs an order-3 block. A page of its own goes back to the page
+ * allocator the moment the skb is freed. Payloads that do not fit in a
+ * page (jumbo MRU) keep using kmalloc.
+ */
+static struct sk_buff *nss_core_alloc_empty_buffer(uint16_t payload_len)
+{
+	struct sk_buff *nbuf;
+	struct page *page;
+
+	if (unlikely(payload_len > SKB_WITH_OVERHEAD(PAGE_SIZE)))
+		return alloc_skb(payload_len, GFP_ATOMIC);
+
+	page = alloc_page(GFP_ATOMIC | __GFP_NOWARN);
+	if (unlikely(!page))
+		return NULL;
+
+	nbuf = build_skb(page_address(page), PAGE_SIZE);
+	if (unlikely(!nbuf)) {
+		__free_page(page);
+		return NULL;
+	}
+
+	return nbuf;
+}
+
+/*
  * nss_core_alloc_max_avail_size_buffers()
  *	Allocate maximum available sized buffers.
  */
@@ -2121,11 +2155,11 @@
 		 * (dev_alloc_skb): a long-held ~2KB payload pins its whole
 		 * 32KB frag chunk once the neighbouring traffic buffers are
 		 * freed, which measured as ~5x RAM amplification on a 512MB
-		 * IPQ8074 (~200MB pinned for ~35MB of payloads). alloc_skb()
-		 * backs each payload with its own kmalloc object instead,
-		 * which is returned in full the moment the skb is freed.
+		 * IPQ8074 (~200MB pinned for ~35MB of payloads). Each payload
+		 * gets memory of its own instead, which is returned in full
+		 * the moment the skb is freed.
 		 */
-		struct sk_buff *nbuf = alloc_skb(payload_len, GFP_ATOMIC);
+		struct sk_buff *nbuf = nss_core_alloc_empty_buffer(payload_len);
 		if (unlikely(!nbuf)) {
 			/*
 			 * ERR:
